# Topic 99: Stateless REST Security with JWT & Method-Level Role Authorization

> 🎯 **Roadmap Seal:** **`[MUST-KNOW / CORE]`** — *Essential modern foundation required for backend roles. Master this thoroughly.*

## 1. What It Is and Why It Exists

Modern enterprise architectures and microservices require horizontally scalable, stateless authentication. In legacy monolithic web systems, user sessions were held in server RAM (`HttpSession`). In distributed cloud environments where hundreds of container instances autoscale dynamically behind a load balancer, sharing sessions across servers requires sticky sessions or shared distributed session caches (e.g. Redis).

**Stateless Authentication with JSON Web Tokens (JWT)** solves this by decentralizing authentication state:
- The server issues a cryptographically signed, immutable token containing identity claims upon successful login.
- On subsequent requests, the client transmits this token inside the `Authorization: Bearer <token>` HTTP header.
- Any backend microservice possessing the signing key can verify token authenticity and extract user roles instantaneously in memory without database lookups or inter-service network queries.

### The JWT Interception Flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Client
    participant Filter as JwtAuthenticationFilter (OncePerRequestFilter)
    participant JwtSvc as JwtService
    participant Context as SecurityContextHolder
    participant Rest as @RestController (@PreAuthorize)

    Client->>Filter: GET /api/v1/admin/dashboard (Header: "Bearer <token>")
    Filter->>JwtSvc: validateToken(token)
    alt Valid Signature & Unexpired
        JwtSvc-->>Filter: Valid claims (Username: sarah, Roles: [ROLE_ADMIN])
        Filter->>Context: setAuthentication(UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken)
        Filter->>Rest: chain.doFilter(request, response)
        Rest->>Rest: Evaluate @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')") -> GRANTED
        Rest-->>Client: 200 OK [Admin Dashboard Data]
    else Invalid Signature / Expired
        Filter->>Client: 401 Unauthorized (Invalid JWT Token)
    end
```

### Real-World Analogy
Consider airport security and concert tickets.
- **Session-Based Security:** The bouncer maintains a giant paper guest list at the door. Every time a guest steps outside and returns, the bouncer must flip through hundreds of pages to find their name. If the bouncer loses the clipboard, nobody can enter.
- **JWT Stateless Security:** The venue gives every guest a holographic wristband (**JWT**) embossed with their name and access tier (**Claims**), sealed with a tamper-evident cryptographic stamp (**Signature**). Any security guard at any gate can verify the holographic seal instantly in 1 millisecond without looking at a central guest list.

---

## 2. Prerequisites
Before studying JWT and method security, ensure you have mastered:
- [Topic 92: Production REST Controller Architecture with Spring Web MVC](#topic-92) — HTTP controllers and request processing.
- [Topic 98: Modern SecurityFilterChain Architecture & Password Encryption](#topic-98) — Servlet filter chains and HttpSecurity DSL.

