# Topic 116: Distributed Scheduling (@Scheduled) & Cluster Locking with ShedLock

> 🚀 **Roadmap Seal:** **`[ADVANCED / DEEP-DIVE]`** — *Advanced enterprise specialization & architecture. Study once core fundamentals are solid.*

## 1. What It Is and Why It Exists

Enterprise backend systems frequently require executing automated background maintenance jobs at regular intervals: running midnight financial reconciliation, generating daily invoices, purging expired user sessions, and syncing currency exchange rates.

Spring Framework provides elementary scheduling via `@Scheduled`. In a local development environment running a single JVM, `@Scheduled` executes smoothly.

### The Multi-Instance Cluster Disaster
In modern cloud architectures (Kubernetes, AWS ECS, Docker Swarm), backend microservices run across multiple container instances (e.g. 5 replica Pods) for high availability. 

When midnight strikes, **all 5 pods trigger the `@Scheduled` job simultaneously!**
- 5 credit card charges are processed for the same customer.
- 5 identical invoices are emailed to the customer.
- Relational database tables experience massive deadlocks as 5 nodes attempt to update the same rows.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph Kubernetes Cluster [5 Pod Replicas Running Concurrently]
        P1["Pod 1 (@Scheduled)"]
        P2["Pod 2 (@Scheduled)"]
        P3["Pod 3 (@Scheduled)"]
    end

    subgraph Distributed Lock Manager [ShedLock / Relational DB]
        L[("shedlock table\n(name, lock_until, locked_at, locked_by)")]
    end

    P1 -->|"1. Acquires Lock (Pod 1 wins)"| L
    P2 -->|"2. Attempts Lock -> REJECTED (Already locked)"| L
    P3 -->|"3. Attempts Lock -> REJECTED (Already locked)"| L

    P1 -->|"4. Executes Midnight Billing"| DB[("Production Database\n(Processed Exactly Once)")]
```

**ShedLock** solves this by establishing a distributed lock (backed by a relational database table or Redis key). When the scheduled trigger fires across all 5 nodes, only the fastest node acquires the distributed lock and executes the job. All other 4 nodes observe the active lock, skip execution, and go back to sleep.

### Real-World Analogy
Consider a conference room with a single whiteboard marker (**The Distributed Lock**).
- **Without Locking:** Five different meeting attendees stand up at the same moment, walk to the board, and scribble on top of each other's writing, ruining the presentation.
- **With ShedLock:** The first person to grab the marker steps to the board. The other four attendees see the marker is taken, sit down, and allow the speaker to finish the presentation cleanly without interruption.

---

## 2. Prerequisites
Before studying distributed scheduling, ensure you have mastered:
- [Topic 51: Executor Framework & Thread Pools](#topic-51) — Timers, delays, and task queues.
- [Topic 90: Auto-Configuration Mechanics, Starters & Embedded Runtimes](#topic-90) — Spring Boot auto-configuration.
- [Topic 115: Asynchronous Task Execution (@Async, ThreadPoolTaskExecutor & CompletableFuture)](#topic-115) — Asynchronous background processing.

---

## 3. Syntax Breakdown & Core Mechanics

### 3.1 Spring Scheduling Constructs

```java
@Configuration
@EnableScheduling
public class SchedulingConfiguration {}
```

#### Trigger Types:
1. **`fixedRate`:** Executes tasks at fixed intervals measured from the **start** time of the previous invocation (`@Scheduled(fixedRate = 5000)`).
2. **`fixedDelay`:** Executes tasks at fixed intervals measured from the **completion** time of the previous invocation (`@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 5000)`).
3. **`cron` Expression (6-fields in Spring):**
   ```
   second minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week
   "0 0 0 * * *"   --> Daily at midnight (00:00:00)
   "0 15 10 * * ?" --> Daily at 10:15 AM
   "0 0/15 * * * *"--> Every 15 minutes
   ```

---

### 3.2 ShedLock Configuration & Lock Attributes

Add dependencies `shedlock-spring` and `shedlock-provider-jdbc-template`:

```sql
-- PostgreSQL / MySQL ShedLock Table Schema
CREATE TABLE shedlock (
    name VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,
    lock_until TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    locked_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    locked_by VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
);
```

```java
@Configuration
@EnableScheduling
@EnableSchedulerLock(defaultLockAtMostFor = "10m")
public class DistributedSchedulerConfig {

    @Bean
    public LockProvider lockProvider(DataSource dataSource) {
        return new JdbcTemplateLockProvider(
            JdbcTemplateLockProvider.Configuration.builder()
                .withJdbcTemplate(new JdbcTemplate(dataSource))
                .usingDbTime() // Prevent node clock skew
                .build()
        );
    }
}
```

---

### 3.3 Annotating Scheduled Jobs with `@SchedulerLock`

```java
@Service
public class BillingReconciliationJob {

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 0 * * *") // Daily at Midnight
    @SchedulerLock(
        name = "dailyBillingReconciliationLock",
        lockAtMostFor = "15m",  // Max lock duration if node dies mid-job
        lockAtLeastFor = "30s"  // Minimum lock duration to prevent fast node re-acquisition
    )
    public void executeDailyBilling() {
        log.info("Executing midnight billing reconciliation on node: {}", HostUtils.getHostName());
        // Business logic executed exactly once across the cluster
    }
}
```

- **`lockAtMostFor`:** Safety net. If the node executing the job crashes, runs out of memory, or gets killed, the lock will automatically expire after this period, allowing other nodes to execute future runs.
- **`lockAtLeastFor`:** If a task executes very quickly (e.g. 500ms), other nodes whose clock is slightly behind might attempt to run the job after the first node releases the lock. Setting `lockAtLeastFor = "30s"` guarantees the lock stays held for at least 30 seconds.

