## 3. Core Document Structure & BSON Types

A MongoDB document is stored in BSON format, supporting rich types like dates, 64-bit integers, and binary ObjectIds:

```json
{
  "_id": ObjectId("655f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d"),
  "sku": "LAPTOP-PRO-16",
  "name": "Developer Workstation 16",
  "price": 1899.99,
  "stock": 42,
  "inStock": true,
  "tags": ["developer", "hardware", "sale"],
  "specs": {
    "cpu": "12 Cores",
    "ramGb": 32,
    "ssdGb": 1024
  },
  "createdAt": ISODate("2026-11-21T10:00:00Z")
}
```

---

## 4. Fundamental CRUD Operations Reference

### 1. Create: `insertOne()` & `insertMany()`
```javascript
// Insert a single document:
db.products.insertOne({
  sku: "KEY-01",
  name: "Mechanical Keyboard",
  price: 89.99,
  stock: 25
});

// Insert multiple documents:
db.products.insertMany([
  { sku: "MOU-02", name: "Wireless Mouse", price: 29.50, stock: 100 },
  { sku: "MON-03", name: "4K Monitor", price: 349.00, stock: 12 }
]);
```

---

### 2. Read: `find()` & Query Operators
```javascript
// Find all products in collection:
db.products.find({});

// Filter by exact match:
db.products.find({ name: "Mechanical Keyboard" });

// Numerical range comparison ($gt, $gte, $lt, $lte):
db.products.find({ price: { $gte: 50.00, $lte: 200.00 } });

// In list ($in):
db.products.find({ sku: { $in: ["KEY-01", "MOU-02"] } });

// Querying nested fields (using dot notation):
db.products.find({ "specs.ramGb": 32 });

// Projection (include only name and price, exclude _id):
db.products.find({}, { name: 1, price: 1, _id: 0 });

// Sorting and Paging:
db.products.find({})
  .sort({ price: -1 }) // 1 = Ascending, -1 = Descending
  .skip(10)
  .limit(10);
```

---

### 3. Update: `updateOne()` & `updateMany()`
Always use update operators like **`$set`** or **`$inc`** to modify specific fields without overwriting the entire document:

```javascript
// Set a field value:
db.products.updateOne(
  { sku: "KEY-01" },
  { $set: { price: 79.99, onSale: true } }
);

// Atomically increment a counter (prevents race conditions):
db.products.updateOne(
  { sku: "KEY-01" },
  { $inc: { stock: -1 } } // Decrements stock by 1
);
```

> [!CAUTION] Never Omit Update Operators!
> In older MongoDB APIs, executing `db.products.update({ sku: "KEY-01" }, { price: 79.99 })` would completely **replace the entire document** with just `{ price: 79.99 }`, erasing all other fields! Always use `$set` or use modern `updateOne()`.

---

### 4. Delete: `deleteOne()` & `deleteMany()`
```javascript
// Delete first document matching criteria:
db.products.deleteOne({ sku: "OBSOLETE-99" });

// Delete all matching documents:
db.products.deleteMany({ stock: 0 });
```

---

## 5. MongoDB Aggregation Pipeline Reference

The **Aggregation Pipeline** is MongoDB's framework for multi-stage analytics, transforming documents sequentially through discrete stages:

```text
Raw Documents ---> [$match Filter] ---> [$group Aggregation] ---> [$sort] ---> Final Output
```

### Realistic Example: Revenue by Category
```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  // Stage 1: Filter paid orders only
  { $match: { status: "PAID" } },

  // Stage 2: Group by customerId and calculate total spend & order count
  {
    $group: {
      _id: "$customerId",
      totalSpent: { $sum: "$totalAmount" },
      orderCount: { $sum: 1 }
    }
  },

  // Stage 3: Filter customers who spent at least $100
  { $match: { totalSpent: { $gte: 100.00 } } },

  // Stage 4: Sort by total spend descending
  { $sort: { totalSpent: -1 } }
]);
```

# Topic 131: Java Persistence Integration: Spring Data JPA vs Spring Data MongoDB

> 🎯 **Roadmap Seal:** **`[MUST-KNOW / CORE]`** — *Essential modern foundation required for backend roles. Master this thoroughly.*

## 1. What It Is and Why It Exists

In modern enterprise applications, developers rarely write low-level JDBC socket code or raw database driver queries by hand. The **Spring Data** umbrella project provides a consistent, high-level programming model that abstracts database access across both relational and non-relational engines.

Whether querying relational MySQL through **Spring Data JPA** or document collections through **Spring Data MongoDB**, Spring Data delivers:
1. Automated CRUD implementation via the **Repository Pattern** (`save()`, `findById()`, `findAll()`, `deleteById()`).
2. Type-safe Java domain entities.
3. Declarative query generation from method names (`findByEmailAndStatus(...)`).
4. Centralized connection pool and driver management in `application.properties`.

---

## 2. Side-by-Side Architectural Comparison

| Dimension | Relational: Spring Data JPA (MySQL) | Document: Spring Data MongoDB |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Underlying Engine** | MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB via JDBC | MongoDB via Reactive / Blocking Mongo Driver |
| **Class Annotation** | **`@Entity`** + **`@Table(name = "products")`** | **`@Document(collection = "products")`** |
| **Primary Key** | `@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = IDENTITY)` | `@Id` (String / BSON `ObjectId`) |
| **Repository Interface** | `extends JpaRepository<Product, Long>` | `extends MongoRepository<Product, String>` |
| **Relationship Model** | `@OneToMany`, `@ManyToOne`, `@JoinColumn` | `@DocumentReference` or Embedded Objects/Lists |
| **Transactions** | `@Transactional` (JDBC / JTA transaction) | `@Transactional` (requires Mongo Replica Set) |

---

## 3. Implementation A: Spring Data JPA (Relational MySQL)

### 1. Maven Dependencies (`pom.xml`)
```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- Spring Data JPA & Hibernate -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!-- MySQL JDBC Driver -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.mysql</groupId>
        <artifactId>mysql-connector-j</artifactId>
        <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

### 2. Configuration (`application.properties`)
```properties
# MySQL Connection Settings
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/enterprise_shop?serverTimezone=UTC&useSSL=false
spring.datasource.username=root
spring.datasource.password=
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver

# Connection Pool (HikariCP)
spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=10
spring.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=5

# Hibernate Settings
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate
spring.jpa.show-sql=false
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true
```

### 3. JPA Domain Entity
```java
package com.handbook.shop.entity;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "products")
public class Product {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true, length = 64)
    private String sku;

    @Column(nullable = false, length = 150)
    private String productName;

    @Column(nullable = false, precision = 10, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal unitPrice;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private int stockQuantity;

    // Constructors, Getters, and Setters...
    public Product() {}

    public Product(String sku, String productName, BigDecimal unitPrice, int stockQuantity) {
        this.sku = sku;
        this.productName = productName;
        this.unitPrice = unitPrice;
        this.stockQuantity = stockQuantity;
    }

    public Long getId() { return id; }
    public String getSku() { return sku; }
    public BigDecimal getUnitPrice() { return unitPrice; }
}
```

### 4. JPA Repository
```java
package com.handbook.shop.repository;

import com.handbook.shop.entity.Product;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;
import java.util.Optional;

@Repository
public interface ProductJpaRepository extends JpaRepository<Product, Long> {
    Optional<Product> findBySku(String sku);
}
```

